{"cuisine/limburgian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/limburgian-cuisine","n":"Limburgian cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Dutch cuisine","cuisine/dutch-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Belgium","intro":[["Limburgisch cuisine, the cuisine of the Province of Limburg, is different from the Belgian and Dutch cuisines but also shares many similarities with these cuisines. Maastricht is known for its Limburgian cuisine."],["Limburg is a little different compared to the rest of the Dutch and Belgian provinces. The landscape is hilly in the southeast (while the rest of the Dutch landscape is cultivated and flat) and the Limburgish language could be said to be a separate language rather than a dialect of Dutch. This different landscape provides the Limburgish cuisine with a lot of game meat, especially in the hunting season. The north of the Limburg is quite flat and is the largest asparagus producing area of the Netherlands. In the southwest is found the Haspengouw which is famous for being the fruit basket of Belgium."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"limburgian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Limburgian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Dutch cuisine","cuisine/dutch-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Dutch cuisine","cuisine/dutch-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine"},"cuisine/australian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/australian-cuisine","n":"Australian cuisine","c":"#55b8ad","crumb":[["Oceania","cuisines#oceania"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Ashmore and Cartier Is.","intro":[["Australian cuisine is the food and cooking practices of Australia and its inhabitants. Australia has absorbed culinary contributions and adaptations from various cultures around the world, including British, European, Asian, and Middle Eastern."],["Indigenous Australians have occupied Australia for some 65,000 years, during which they developed a unique hunter-gatherer diet, known as bush tucker, drawn from regional Australian plants and animals. Australia became a collection of British colonies from 1788 to 1900, during which time culinary tastes were strongly influenced by British and Irish migrants, with agricultural products such as beef cattle, sheep, and wheat becoming staples in the local diet. The Australian gold rushes introduced more varied immigrants and cuisines, mainly Italian and Chinese, whilst post-war immigration programs led to a large-scale diversification of local food, mainly due to the influence of migrants from the Mediterranean, East Asia and South Asia."],["Australian cuisine in the 21st century reflects the influence of globalisation, with many fast-food restaurants and international trends becoming influential. Organic and biodynamic foods have also become widely available alongside a revival of interest in bush tucker. Australia exports many agricultural products, including cattle, sheep, poultry, milk, vegetables, fruit, nuts, wheat, barley, and canola. Australia also produces wine, beer and soft drinks."],["While fast food chains are abundant, Australia's metropolitan areas have restaurants that offer both local and international foods. Restaurants which include contemporary adaptations, interpretations or fusions of exotic influences are frequently termed Modern Australian."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"australian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Belly Rumbles: Australian Recipes","ref":"site/bellyrumbles-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"bellyrumbles.com","note":"Sara McCleary's collection of Australian classics such as Anzac biscuits, pavlova, sausage rolls and native-ingredient bakes.","url":"https://bellyrumbles.com/category/australian-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"RecipeTin Eats","ref":"site/recipetineats-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"recipetineats.com","note":"Sydney-based Nagi Maehashi's widely used recipe site; broad in scope, but a staple of Australian home cooking.","url":"https://www.recipetineats.com/home/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"The Cook's Companion","ref":"cookbook/the-cook-s-companion","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Stephanie Alexander, 2004","note":"Ingredient-by-ingredient reference long regarded as the standard Australian kitchen book; a 30th anniversary edition followed.","url":"https://www.penguin.com.au/books/the-cooks-companion-9781920989002","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781920989002"},{"name":"Mabu Mabu","ref":"cookbook/mabu-mabu","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Nornie Bero, 2021","note":"Home cooking with native Australian ingredients by Torres Strait Islander chef Nornie Bero.","url":"https://publishing.hardiegrant.com/en-us/books/mabu-mabu-by-nornie-bero/9781743797280","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781743797280"}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":9,"links":[["Anzac biscuit","dish/anzac-biscuit"],["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"],["Flake (fish)","dish/flake-fish"],["Kangaroo meat","dish/kangaroo-meat"],["Parrot pie","dish/parrot-pie"],["Pavlova","dish/pavlova"],["Sausage sizzle","dish/sausage-sizzle"],["Steak and eggs","dish/steak-and-eggs"],["Zucchini slice","dish/zucchini-slice"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Golden syrup","ingredient/golden-syrup"],["Rolled oats","ingredient/rolled-oats"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Atlantic cod","ingredient/atlantic-cod"],["Fish","ingredient/fish"],["French fries","ingredient/french-fries"],["Fried fish","ingredient/fried-fish"],["Fries","ingredient/fries"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Shark","ingredient/shark"],["Bird meat","ingredient/bird-meat"],["Parrot","ingredient/parrot"],["Egg white","ingredient/egg-white"],["Sucrose","ingredient/sucrose"],["Sausage","ingredient/sausage"],["Sliced bread","ingredient/sliced-bread"],["Beefsteak","ingredient/beefsteak"],["Zucchini","ingredient/zucchini"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Within Australian cooking","count":3,"links":[["Australian Chinese cuisine","cuisine/australian-chinese-cuisine"],["Bush tucker","cuisine/bush-tucker"],["Tasmanian cuisine","cuisine/tasmanian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"More from Ashmore and Cartier Is.","count":1,"links":[["Christmas Island cuisine","cuisine/christmas-island-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Oceanic cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/6/61/Australia_Brunch.jpg/500px-Australia_Brunch.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Lachlan Hardy from Sydney, Australia","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Australia_Brunch.jpg"}},"dish/carpaccio":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/carpaccio","n":"Carpaccio","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Italian cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Carpaccio is a dish of meat or fish (such as beef, veal, venison, salmon or tuna), thinly sliced or pounded thin, and served raw, typically as an appetiser. It was invented in 1950 by Giuseppe Cipriani, founder of Harry's Bar in Venice, Italy, and popularised during the second half of the twentieth century. The beef was served with lemon, olive oil and white truffle or Parmesan cheese. Later, the term was extended to dishes containing other raw meats or fish, thinly sliced and served with lemon or vinegar, olive oil, salt and ground pepper."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-carpaccio"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carpaccio"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Pasta Grannies","ref":"site/pastagrannies-substack-com","color":"#55b8ad","sh